{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795714491,"nonce":"vsSiyxm6WDGoXB0j+bBalIKbWvk=","title":"An explicit or IMEX fvm march recompiles at every new coupling window, and about twice as slowly when it carries a flux quadrature","message":"**What goes wrong.** A participant that marches window after window (a coupling run step by step, `compile=False`, or the first window of a compiled one) should compile its step once and reuse it. Measured through the participant's `march()` on four successive windows, each window compiles again: about 8 XLA compiles and 2.7 to 3.3 s per window with `erk(\"ssprk3\", cfl=0.5)`; with `flux=` (the stage-quadrature flux mean) 7 compiles and 5.2 to 5.9 s per window. When only the data changes and the window stays the same, nothing recompiles (0 compiles, 0.03 to 0.06 s). So the trigger is the new window, not the data.\n\n**Reproduce.** Measured on the unmerged branch named in the label, CPU, float64, with `jax.log_compiles` counting \"Compiling\" records. The strip of `test_an_explicit_march_hands_over_the_window_mean_flux_of_its_own_stages` (tests/test_coupling_participants.py, `_heated_strip(9)`), then for `w in range(4)`:\n\n```python\npart.march(prob, state, 0.04 * w, 0.04 * (w + 1), data, time=erk(\"ssprk3\", cfl=0.5))\npart.march(..., flux=[(expr, \"left\", left)])      # the same, with the flux quadrature\n```\n\nWindow 0 compiles about 136 (first use); windows 1 to 3 repeat the figures above. With no `cfl=` (`erk(\"ssprk3\")`) it is 8 compiles and 2.9 s without `flux=`, 7 compiles and 6.3 to 6.8 s with it. An IMEX sender with `cfl=` and data changing each window: 7 compiles per window, 13 s without and 18 s with `flux=`. I did not time a whole eager coupling run; its windows call this same method.\n\n**Expected.** Window 1 and later reuse window 0's compiled step: no compiles, the 0.05 s of the data-only case.\n\n**Where.** `window_block` (jno/utils/solver/backend_blocks.py) says compiled time-scheme pieces are shared across windows; the `jit(run)` of `ERKScheme.integrate` / `IMEXScheme.integrate` is still compiled again. With `flux=` the cache is also bypassed on purpose (`cache = {} if traced or flux is not None`), because the flux closure holds the window's data, so the stepper is rebuilt on every call.\n\n**Workaround.** Compiled coupling windows (the default where the problems are autonomous) avoid it.\n\n**Direction.** Find what differs between windows in the cache key or the copied block. For the flux, pass the window data as an argument of the jitted step instead of closing over it, so one compiled step serves every window.","files":null}]}